{"message_id":"f61ba05b-3464-4f8f-8bd6-343610fd524a","timestamp":"2026-05-13T11:23:21.493558Z","version":"1.0","source_id":"jarvis","event_type":"result","project":null,"correlation_id":"c30f8b2c-e4f3-4764-9018-5ee0573d560e","payload":{"command":"chat","result":{"response":"## Alignment Judgment: **Misaligned** (confidence: 95%)\n\n### Why\n\nADR-ARCH-001's core invariant is that **cloud LLMs are kept out of the supervisor's hot path entirely**. The existing design allows cloud escalation only through the `escalate_to_frontier` tool — which is **user-initiated and attended-only**. This is a deliberate boundary: the supervisor never makes autonomous decisions to route user requests to cloud models.\n\nThe proposed change introduces **supervisor-initiated automatic escalation** driven by the local reasoner's confidence score. This directly contradicts two parts of ADR-ARCH-001:\n\n1. **The local-first hot-path invariant** — the supervisor would no longer be the sole decision-maker in the hot path for safety-critical requests; a cloud model would be invoked automatically without user consent.\n2. **The attended-only constraint** — cloud escalation would become an internal mechanism rather than an explicit user choice, removing the human-in-the-loop gate.\n\n### What would need to change\n\n- **ADR-ARCH-001** would need to be amended (or a new ADR created) to redefine the \"attended-only\" boundary — explicitly permitting supervisor-triggered cloud escalation under bounded, auditable conditions (e.g., confidence thresholds, budget caps, safety-critical classification).\n- **The supervisor's contract** would need to be updated to define:\n  - What constitutes \"low confidence\" (measurable signal from the local reasoner?).\n  - What qualifies as \"safety-critical\" or \"high-stakes\" (classification taxonomy?).\n  - How the per-session budget cap is enforced and audited.\n  - Whether the user is notified before the escalation fires (partial attended-ness).\n\nWithout these changes, the proposal sits outside ADR-ARCH-001's design envelope. The architect's judgment is clear: **this is a design-level change, not a small tweak** — it would require an ADR update before implementation.","tools_called":[],"correlation_id":"c30f8b2c-e4f3-4764-9018-5ee0573d560e"},"correlation_id":"c30f8b2c-e4f3-4764-9018-5ee0573d560e","success":true}}
{"message_id":"cd323e78-2bb3-4c50-a171-c1d12dc04e7d","timestamp":"2026-05-13T11:23:26.031867Z","version":"1.0","source_id":"jarvis","event_type":"result","project":null,"correlation_id":"0f67cefa-50b0-4c0e-98fa-179aba14e725","payload":{"command":"chat","result":{"response":"{\n  \"follow_ups\": [\n    \"Can you draft the updated ADR-ARCH-001 (or a new ADR) that would permit supervisor-triggered cloud escalation under bounded conditions?\",\n    \"What would a practical confidence-threshold mechanism look like for the local reasoner to signal 'low confidence'?\",\n    \"How should the per-session budget cap be enforced technically — at the tool level, the supervisor level, or via a separate budget tracker?\",\n    \"If we keep the attended-only constraint, what design changes would let us get some of the safety benefits of automatic escalation without violating the ADR?\",\n    \"Are there any existing ADRs or architectural decisions that touch on safety-critical request classification that we should align with?\"\n  ]\n}","tools_called":[],"correlation_id":"0f67cefa-50b0-4c0e-98fa-179aba14e725"},"correlation_id":"0f67cefa-50b0-4c0e-98fa-179aba14e725","success":true}}
